1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,640
 Hello I'm Russell and welcome to the Articulate Journal where I'm exploring what it takes to build

2
00:00:06,640 --> 00:00:12,320
 an AI native architecture from first principles through the design and implementation of a reference

3
00:00:12,320 --> 00:00:18,720
 architecture for architectural intelligence. This isn't a tutorial series or a product demo,

4
00:00:18,720 --> 00:00:23,680
 it's a public engineering journal documenting the architectural decisions, experiments,

5
00:00:23,680 --> 00:00:29,760
 and lessons learned as the architecture evolves. In the previous episode I explored the idea of

6
00:00:29,760 --> 00:00:35,040
 treating architecture as knowledge. That information exists across different architectural domains.

7
00:00:36,320 --> 00:00:41,600
 The business capability may be supported by application components. Those components may

8
00:00:41,600 --> 00:00:48,720
 depend on data, integrations, and infrastructure. Decisions explain why parts of the architecture

9
00:00:48,720 --> 00:00:55,440
 exist in their current form. Principles influence those decisions. System characteristics provide

10
00:00:55,440 --> 00:01:02,320
 constraints that shape the resulting design. The knowledge model gives Articulate a way to

11
00:01:02,320 --> 00:01:08,560
 represent this information and importantly the relationships between it, but representing knowledge

12
00:01:08,560 --> 00:01:16,240
 creates another question. How should AI reason with that knowledge? This is where the problem

13
00:01:16,240 --> 00:01:22,960
 becomes more interesting than simply retrieving information. Imagine asking Articulate a question.

14
00:01:24,480 --> 00:01:30,800
 What would be affected if we replaced our current messaging platform? Finding information about

15
00:01:30,800 --> 00:01:35,600
 the messaging platform is only the beginning. To provide a useful architectural answer,

16
00:01:35,600 --> 00:01:42,240
 the system may need to identify which application components depend on it. It may then need to

17
00:01:42,240 --> 00:01:49,520
 understand which business capabilities those components support. There may be architectural

18
00:01:49,520 --> 00:01:55,280
 decisions that explain why the messaging platform was originally selected. Those decisions may have

19
00:01:55,280 --> 00:02:03,120
 been influenced by particular system characteristics or architectural principles. A proposed replacement

20
00:02:03,120 --> 00:02:08,880
 may satisfy some of those constraints while conflicting with others. The answer therefore

21
00:02:08,880 --> 00:02:14,080
 does not exist as a single piece of information waiting to be retrieved. It has to be constructed

22
00:02:14,080 --> 00:02:21,520
 from the knowledge available. This creates an important distinction. Retrieval gathers relevant

23
00:02:21,520 --> 00:02:26,160
 knowledge. Reasoning determines what that knowledge means in the context of the question.

24
00:02:27,280 --> 00:02:32,880
 This distinction is important when thinking about how AI systems interact with architectural knowledge.

25
00:02:34,880 --> 00:02:41,280
 Retrieval Augmented Generation, or RAG, has become a common architectural pattern for giving

26
00:02:41,280 --> 00:02:47,280
 language models access to information beyond their training data. A typical implementation

27
00:02:47,840 --> 00:02:54,560
 takes documents, divides them into chunks, creates vector embeddings, and retrieves chunks that are

28
00:02:54,560 --> 00:03:00,960
 semantically similar to the user's question. This is useful. Articulate may use semantic

29
00:03:00,960 --> 00:03:06,960
 search for exactly this reason. But architectural knowledge is not simply a collection of text

30
00:03:06,960 --> 00:03:13,760
 fragments. Consider the following question. Which business capabilities would be affected

31
00:03:13,760 --> 00:03:20,320
 if this infrastructure component failed? The answer is not necessarily contained within a

32
00:03:20,320 --> 00:03:27,120
 semantically similar paragraph. The system may need to follow relationships. An infrastructure

33
00:03:27,120 --> 00:03:33,680
 component hosts an application component. That application component supports a business capability.

34
00:03:34,960 --> 00:03:38,640
 The relationship between those concepts itself is part of the knowledge.

35
00:03:40,480 --> 00:03:45,600
 Semantic similarity can help us find relevant information, but it cannot be the only way

36
00:03:45,600 --> 00:03:52,000
 we navigate architectural knowledge. This is one of the reasons, such as approaches like RAG,

37
00:03:52,720 --> 00:03:59,200
 graph RAG are interesting. Rather than treating information only as isolated chunks of text,

38
00:03:59,200 --> 00:04:03,680
 a graph allows relationships between information to become part of the retrieval process.

39
00:04:04,960 --> 00:04:09,360
 This aligns naturally with the knowledge model introduced in the previous episode.

40
00:04:11,040 --> 00:04:17,520
 If articulate knows that, a business capability is supported by an application component.

41
00:04:17,520 --> 00:04:23,840
 An application component depends on an integration. That integration uses a messaging platform.

42
00:04:24,400 --> 00:04:30,160
 The messaging platform is posted on particular infrastructure. Then a question about infrastructure

43
00:04:30,160 --> 00:04:36,240
 can be explored through those relationships. The important shift is that retrieval is no longer

44
00:04:36,240 --> 00:04:41,200
 only about finding information that looks similar to the question. It can also be about

45
00:04:41,200 --> 00:04:48,480
 navigating the structure of what is known. However, graph traversal is not the answer to every question

46
00:04:48,480 --> 00:04:55,520
 either. Some questions are semantic, some are relational, some are structured. Others may require

47
00:04:55,520 --> 00:05:00,800
 several of these approaches together. The important architectural principle, therefore,

48
00:05:00,800 --> 00:05:07,840
 is not that articulate should use vector search, knowledge graph or structured queries. It is that

49
00:05:07,840 --> 00:05:13,760
 different questions require different ways of retrieving knowledge. This leads to a broader

50
00:05:13,760 --> 00:05:19,760
 question. Before articulate can decide how to retrieve information, it needs to understand

51
00:05:19,760 --> 00:05:26,720
 what kind of question it is trying to answer. Consider these questions. What depends on this

52
00:05:26,720 --> 00:05:33,280
 component? Why did we choose this technology? Does this proposal conflict with an architectural

53
00:05:33,280 --> 00:05:40,160
 principle? What changed between these two architectural states? What evidence supports this claim?

54
00:05:41,680 --> 00:05:47,360
 Each question requires something different from the system. The first requires understanding

55
00:05:47,360 --> 00:05:53,200
 relationships and dependencies. The second requires reconstructing the context around a decision.

56
00:05:54,160 --> 00:06:00,960
 The third requires evaluating a proposal against existing constraints. The fourth requires reasoning

57
00:06:00,960 --> 00:06:07,600
 across time. The fifth requires tracing knowledge back to supporting evidence. Calling all of this

58
00:06:07,600 --> 00:06:15,840
 simply agent reasoning hides an important part of the architecture. The system needs different

59
00:06:15,840 --> 00:06:23,600
 reasoning capabilities. The first is relational reasoning and explores how architectural concepts

60
00:06:23,600 --> 00:06:30,320
 are connected. It allows questions such as what depends on this? What does this support?

61
00:06:31,040 --> 00:06:33,840
 Which parts of the architecture are connected to this decision?

62
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:41,040
 This form of reasoning naturally relies heavily on the relationships captured with inside the

63
00:06:41,040 --> 00:06:49,520
 knowledge model. Architecture contains constraints, principles, system characteristics, policies,

64
00:06:50,080 --> 00:06:55,360
 and previous decisions, all influence what constitutes as an appropriate design.

65
00:06:56,560 --> 00:07:03,280
 Constraint reasoning asks questions such as does this proposal violate an existing architectural

66
00:07:03,280 --> 00:07:10,720
 principle? Does this design satisfy the required available characteristic? The system is not simply

67
00:07:10,720 --> 00:07:16,000
 looking for relevant information. It is evaluating one piece of knowledge in the context of another.

68
00:07:17,120 --> 00:07:23,520
 Architectural decisions frequently involve alternatives. Comparative reasoning examines

69
00:07:23,520 --> 00:07:29,600
 options in the context of the problem being solved. The important comparison is rarely

70
00:07:29,600 --> 00:07:35,840
 a generic list of advantages and disadvantages. An option may be appropriate in one architectural

71
00:07:35,840 --> 00:07:43,360
 context and unsuitable in another. The reasoning therefore needs to consider the requirements,

72
00:07:43,360 --> 00:07:48,000
 characteristics, principles, and the existing architecture surrounding that decision.

73
00:07:49,440 --> 00:07:55,440
 Architecture evolves. A decision that is correct today may replace an earlier decision that was

74
00:07:55,440 --> 00:08:04,160
 equally correct when it was made. Temporal reasoning allows articulate to understand that

75
00:08:04,160 --> 00:08:10,080
 architectural knowledge exists within time. It can help answer questions such as

76
00:08:11,280 --> 00:08:17,680
 what did this decision replace? Why did the architecture change? Which assumptions were

77
00:08:17,680 --> 00:08:23,200
 true when this decision was made? This becomes increasingly important as the knowledge model

78
00:08:23,200 --> 00:08:30,880
 evolves. Architectural knowledge should not exist without context. Claims may be supported by

79
00:08:31,440 --> 00:08:38,080
 conversations, documents, observations, and other evidence. Evidential reasoning allows

80
00:08:38,080 --> 00:08:44,240
 the system to ask which supports this conclusion, where did this information come from, and how

81
00:08:44,240 --> 00:08:50,480
 confidence should we be in it? This is particularly important for an AI-native architecture system

82
00:08:50,480 --> 00:08:56,400
 because not everything discovered or inferred by AI should ultimately become accepted architectural

83
00:08:56,400 --> 00:09:04,240
 truth. These are not necessarily five agents. That distinction matters. These are reasoning

84
00:09:04,240 --> 00:09:10,320
 capabilities. How these capabilities are eventually implemented is a separate architectural decision.

85
00:09:11,440 --> 00:09:16,960
 This is where the idea of a genetic rag becomes useful. Instead of following a fixed retrieval

86
00:09:16,960 --> 00:09:22,080
 pipeline, an agent can decide how knowledge should be gathered based on the question it's trying to

87
00:09:22,080 --> 00:09:28,400
 answer. A reasoning process might begin with semantic search to identify relevant concepts.

88
00:09:29,040 --> 00:09:34,080
 It might then traverse relationships within the knowledge model. It could then use structured

89
00:09:34,080 --> 00:09:40,320
 queries to retrieve specific architectural information. The results may reveal that additional

90
00:09:40,320 --> 00:09:46,560
 knowledge is required, causing the reasoning process to perform further retrieval. The important

91
00:09:46,560 --> 00:09:53,120
 difference here is that retrieval is no longer a single step before generation. Retrieval becomes

92
00:09:53,120 --> 00:09:59,440
 part of the reasoning process. The system can decide what information it needs, retrieve it,

93
00:09:59,440 --> 00:10:03,520
 examine what it has discovered, and determine where the further knowledge is required.

94
00:10:04,880 --> 00:10:10,720
 But I think even the term "agentic rag" risks making retrieval the center of the architecture.

95
00:10:12,880 --> 00:10:17,520
 For articulate, the more useful concept is a knowledge reasoning layer.

96
00:10:19,280 --> 00:10:24,640
 The knowledge model is responsible for representing what articulate knows about the architecture.

97
00:10:26,800 --> 00:10:31,760
 The knowledge reasoning layer is responsible for helping articulate reason with that knowledge.

98
00:10:32,880 --> 00:10:38,400
 Conceptually, it sits between reasoning capabilities and the underlying ways in which

99
00:10:38,400 --> 00:10:44,160
 knowledge can be accessed. This is a conceptual boundary rather than an implementation design.

100
00:10:45,680 --> 00:10:51,840
 The purpose is to separate two concerns. The knowledge model represents architectural knowledge.

101
00:10:52,400 --> 00:10:57,840
 The reasoning layer determines how that knowledge should be explored and interpreted for a particular

102
00:10:57,840 --> 00:11:04,480
 problem. This means an agent should not necessarily need to understand how every piece of knowledge

103
00:11:04,480 --> 00:11:10,880
 is stored. Instead, it needs capabilities that allow it to ask meaningful questions of the knowledge

104
00:11:10,880 --> 00:11:18,080
 model. The details of whether those questions require semantic retrieval, graph traversal,

105
00:11:18,080 --> 00:11:23,920
 structured queries, or a combination of approaches belong within the knowledge reasoning layer.

106
00:11:25,920 --> 00:11:31,360
 One of the lessons I'm beginning to take from designing articulate is that starting with agents

107
00:11:31,360 --> 00:11:37,040
 may be the wrong level of abstraction. It's easy to identify a problem and then immediately create

108
00:11:37,040 --> 00:11:44,240
 an agent for it. A research agent, a decision agent, an impact analysis agent, and a principal agent.

109
00:11:45,280 --> 00:11:51,280
 But the existence of an agent tells us very little about the reasoning it actually needs to perform.

110
00:11:52,080 --> 00:11:59,120
 Before designing an agent, I think the more useful questions are, the old age, what problem is it

111
00:11:59,120 --> 00:12:04,800
 trying to solve? What kind of reasoning does that problem require? What knowledge does that

112
00:12:04,800 --> 00:12:10,400
 reasoning depend upon? How should that knowledge be retrieved and what evidence should support the

113
00:12:10,400 --> 00:12:17,280
 result? Only then does it make sense to ask how the capability should be implemented and whether

114
00:12:17,280 --> 00:12:23,920
 an agent is the appropriate runtime abstraction. This follows a wider architectural approach I'm

115
00:12:23,920 --> 00:12:29,040
 trying to take throughout this project. The architecture should emerge from the problem and

116
00:12:29,040 --> 00:12:35,200
 the capabilities required to solve it. The agents can come later. There is another idea

117
00:12:35,200 --> 00:12:40,080
 that becomes possible once knowledge and reasoning are treated separately. The knowledge model can

118
00:12:40,080 --> 00:12:45,920
 tell articulate what it knows, but it does not necessarily explain how a particular conclusion

119
00:12:45,920 --> 00:12:51,440
 was reached. A reasoning process may traverse these relationships and conclude that changing

120
00:12:51,440 --> 00:12:58,240
 the messaging platform could affect a specific business capability. The relationship exists in

121
00:12:58,240 --> 00:13:03,040
 the knowledge model, but the path taken through those relationships is part of the reasoning process.

122
00:13:04,080 --> 00:13:10,320
 This suggests a distinction between a knowledge graph and a reasoning map. The knowledge graph

123
00:13:10,320 --> 00:13:16,000
 represents the connected architectural knowledge available to the system. A reasoning graph represents

124
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:22,080
 the path taken through the knowledge while answering a particular question. The reasoning map is not

125
00:13:22,080 --> 00:13:27,040
 necessarily part of the permanent architectural knowledge. It is an artifact of reasoning,

126
00:13:27,600 --> 00:13:32,800
 but it may become valuable. It provides a way to inspect how a conclusion was reached.

127
00:13:33,680 --> 00:13:39,360
 It can show which knowledge was used and it can expose assumptions. It may allow

128
00:13:39,360 --> 00:13:44,800
 an architect to challenge a reasoning path rather than simply accepting or rejecting the final answer.

129
00:13:45,760 --> 00:13:51,040
 This becomes particularly important when AI is participating in architectural decisions.

130
00:13:51,920 --> 00:13:59,040
 A useful answer is important. Understanding how the system reached that answer may be just as

131
00:13:59,040 --> 00:14:04,240
 important. The knowledge model establishes the foundation for representing architecture

132
00:14:04,240 --> 00:14:11,200
 as connected knowledge. Knowledge reasoning adds the next layer. Articulate needs to do more

133
00:14:11,200 --> 00:14:15,840
 than retrieve information that appears relevant to a question. It needs to understand relationships.

134
00:14:16,400 --> 00:14:22,000
 It needs to evaluate constraints. It needs to compare alternatives within the architectural

135
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:28,320
 context. It needs to reason across time and it needs to understand the evidence behind what it

136
00:14:28,320 --> 00:14:35,920
 knows. And it needs to explain how it reached its conclusions. Semantic search, graph traversal,

137
00:14:35,920 --> 00:14:41,920
 and structured queries remain useful. A Gentic RAG provides a useful model for allowing agents

138
00:14:41,920 --> 00:14:47,520
 to choose between those retrieval strategies dynamically. But none of them individually

139
00:14:47,520 --> 00:14:53,360
 are architectural intelligence. Architectural intelligence emerges from combining structured

140
00:14:53,360 --> 00:14:59,680
 knowledge with explicit reasoning capabilities. If articulate is going to contain agents that

141
00:14:59,680 --> 00:15:04,880
 perform these reasoning capabilities, how should their intended behaviour be described?

142
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:12,320
 This is where DCL enters the architecture, a topic for the next episode. If you're interested

143
00:15:12,320 --> 00:15:17,040
 in AI-native architecture, architectural knowledge, or building systems at reason,

144
00:15:17,040 --> 00:15:21,760
 rather than simply retrieve, I hope you join me for the next episode.

